data.content:
Yes! MSTest 3.8+ introduced **generic `TestDataRow<T1, T2, ...>`** which eliminates the `object[]` boxing:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[DynamicData(nameof(TestData))]
public void Validate_ReturnsExpected(string input, bool expected)
{
    var result = Validator.IsValid(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result);
}

public static IEnumerable<TestDataRow<string, bool>> TestData =>
[
    new("valid@email.com", true),
    new("invalid", false),
    new("", false),
];
```

**Key differences:**
- `IEnumerable<TestDataRow<string, bool>>` instead of `IEnumerable<object[]>`
- Compile-time type checking — mismatched types are caught at build time, not at runtime
- Generic variants exist up to `TestDataRow<T1, ..., T16>`

Requires **MSTest 3.8+** (`Microsoft.Testing.Framework`). If you're on an older version, `object[]` is still the only option with `DynamicData`.
